21:56.543 --> 22:05.950
[SPEAKER_01]: describe the terrain in that area, so let's say you're flying north and you have like a series of ridges. If you look in the map, they all look a bit

22:06.010 --> 22:08.852
[SPEAKER_01]: like dragons,

22:09.313 --> 22:18.620
[SPEAKER_01]: with arms and limbs sticking out. And on the left, you have the drop-off, which is the Western Ghats. So there's this entire drop-off from the

22:18.660 --> 22:27.942
[SPEAKER_01]: plateau. And then you can see in the distance, all the way to the coast on a clear day. So, in between all the ridges, they're all dams, so you have

22:27.982 --> 22:36.225
[SPEAKER_01]: lakes. So, I like to call it the seven lake tour because in these hundred kilometers, there are seven prominent lakes that you see one after the

22:36.305 --> 22:45.548
[SPEAKER_01]: other. So, I mean, I don't know if that gives you a kind of idea of the terrain. So, you've got the, you know, lines of ridges. In between,

22:45.588 --> 22:54.184
[SPEAKER_01]: you've got flatlands where you've got lakes, you've got dams. So it's really interesting, really scenic. As long as you're high, you know,

22:54.224 --> 23:01.430
[SPEAKER_01]: there's not a care in the world. If you get low and close to the terrain, you have to be very, very aware of which direction the wind is coming

23:01.490 --> 23:09.856
[SPEAKER_01]: from. Because normally during season in the morning, you've got east winds. And, you know, by late afternoon, it kind of switches and the sea

23:09.896 --> 23:18.162
[SPEAKER_01]: wind starts pushing in. So, you know, there's after a certain period, you can even have west winds kicking in. uh which are not very good for

23:18.182 --> 23:28.447
[SPEAKER_01]: thermaling but sometimes they kick up um you know huge periods of restitution where you know you can just you know be floating around at

23:28.747 --> 23:38.669
[SPEAKER_01]: you know two two and a half thousand three thousand meters uh you know not having to thermal you know it's just amazing Free lift up to 2,000-3,000

23:38.769 --> 23:50.374
[SPEAKER_00]: meters. And geographically, Panjpani itself is actually a cluster of, I mean, Panjpani means five villages. And also in that area, there are

23:50.394 --> 23:59.999
[SPEAKER_01]: five distinct tablelands. So each tableland is like, you know, it's like a volcanic, you know, plateau, you know, completely flat at the

24:00.059 --> 24:11.879
[SPEAKER_01]: top. And we use it a lot for top landings. However, during the time of a switch, you see dust levels. So that is something you have to be very

24:11.919 --> 24:20.942
[SPEAKER_01]: careful of. So yes, you do get dust levels, but they are always visible. And you can choose to avoid them and fly around it for sure. Because they

24:20.962 --> 24:30.772
[SPEAKER_01]: happen in the most thermic part of the day. So you can choose to fly on instead of landing in a dust level. Makes all the more sense. Landing in a

24:30.812 --> 24:38.439
[SPEAKER_00]: dust devil is not the plan for any pilot, I guess. But, you know, while we are talking about challenges, what is the most common mess up that you

24:38.459 --> 24:45.946
[SPEAKER_00]: see a foreign or a visiting pilot make? Like, of course, the ones who fly there often enough know the red flags. But what is something that can

24:46.006 --> 24:53.373
[SPEAKER_00]: catch you off guard as someone who has just come there for the first or the second time and doesn't really know what the nature might throw at you?

24:55.086 --> 25:03.771
[SPEAKER_01]: So we've had one incident so far, you know, in the last pre-World Cup, which I mentioned to you in 2019, you know, on the very first day of the

25:03.811 --> 25:14.398
[SPEAKER_01]: competition, we had a trial day and we had one of the top ranking Korean pilots who, you know, had a crash and, you know, unfortunately, you

25:14.418 --> 25:23.803
[SPEAKER_01]: know, passed away. I think the main reason was the wind, you know, he didn't, he was too close to the terrain. He was low and he did not account

25:23.843 --> 25:31.789
[SPEAKER_01]: for the wind factor. So, you know, he got into the Lee and, you know, crashed his glider. Unfortunately, he didn't have enough time to

25:31.829 --> 25:41.862
[SPEAKER_01]: deploy the reserve. So I think that's a pretty extreme situation. I think it's the only treaty that's happened in the region in, you know,

25:41.882 --> 25:51.472
[SPEAKER_01]: the 10 years that I've been flying there. So, so I think some no-nos are, you know, unless you really know the area well, and you've understood

25:51.693 --> 26:00.239
[SPEAKER_01]: exactly what's happening with the hydrology in the region, don't get close to the terrain. So if you get below, I mean, we like to put it this way

26:00.279 --> 26:08.642
[SPEAKER_01]: that if you get below 1800 meters, start looking for a safe landing. You know, don't start, you know, trying to scratch around. Don't get close

26:08.662 --> 26:16.284
[SPEAKER_01]: to the terrain unless you really know what you're doing. If you're 100% sure that, yes, the wind is coming back on and you're not in the lee side,

26:16.764 --> 26:23.346
[SPEAKER_01]: then go for it. You know, then scratch, do whatever you want. But if you don't know where you are, you don't know what the wind is doing at that

26:23.386 --> 26:32.469
[SPEAKER_01]: point of time, it's quite possible. If you're high up, you can see in one valley, the wind is coming from the east. In the other valley, you'll see

26:32.509 --> 26:43.725
[SPEAKER_01]: some smoke blowing from the west. And it can be very confusing because it's all thermic air. See what happens as the day progresses. You know,

26:43.805 --> 26:51.351
[SPEAKER_01]: we all know that, you know, rocks take some time to heat up. But once they've, you know, they've heated up, they start pulling up strong

26:51.411 --> 26:58.797
[SPEAKER_01]: thermals. So what tends to happen is that if you can imagine like a volcanic plateau, you know, something like, I don't know, what are the

26:58.837 --> 27:04.882
[SPEAKER_01]: famous ones? I think there's one in Australia. I don't know what it's called. I think the one in America is called Devil's Rock or something

27:04.922 --> 27:14.970
[SPEAKER_01]: like that, where it has this method with a flat top. So what happens in a situation like this is all the sides which are volcanic have kind of

27:15.030 --> 27:22.259
[SPEAKER_01]: absorbed heat. You know, let's say all morning it's been absorbing heat from the east. And, you know, in the afternoon, let's say around 2, 2.30,

27:23.801 --> 27:31.265
[SPEAKER_01]: the predominant wind switches to the west. What's going to happen is that you're getting wind from the west. The rocks, which have heated up

27:31.305 --> 27:38.268
[SPEAKER_01]: all morning in the east, are still throwing up thermals. And they're sucking up all the wind from the valley below. So it's quite possible

27:38.328 --> 27:45.752
[SPEAKER_01]: that you'll see in that valley, you're going to see wind, the smoke blowing from the east and getting pulled towards these rocks. On the

27:45.772 --> 27:54.796
[SPEAKER_01]: west side, it's the predominant sea breeze that's pushing wind from the west. And if you are in a confusing situation like this, you should look

27:54.836 --> 28:04.071
[SPEAKER_01]: for a safe landing. So if you don't know where you are, if you don't know where the wind is from, if you go below 1800, you know, the terrain, the

28:04.091 --> 28:13.997
[SPEAKER_01]: mountaintops are around 11, 1200. So start looking for safe landings is what I feel. That's quite exciting the way you explain it. So basically

28:14.097 --> 28:25.022
[SPEAKER_00]: anything below 5600 AGL, that means that is the cutoff line for you. You should not really try to push it. Yeah, if you're a newbie in the area,

28:25.042 --> 28:32.585
[SPEAKER_01]: definitely, you know, and play it safe when you get there till you understand, you know, exactly what's happening with the wind and the

28:32.645 --> 28:42.823
[SPEAKER_01]: tunnels. So either it's like get high and stay high. If not, just don't put all the eggs in the one basket and then try and look for a landing.

28:42.903 --> 28:52.049
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes sense. Yeah. Makes sense. You know, I was just looking at the competition page on civil comps, and there was a lot of equipment

28:52.069 --> 28:59.034
[SPEAKER_00]: recommendations. Now, I do understand a lot of these come with the legislation that the PwC committee has put on. But usually, what kind of

28:59.094 --> 29:07.540
[SPEAKER_00]: equipment is recommended to fly here? Because I believe the land is really barren and You know, stony at times, considering it's a volcanic

29:07.580 --> 29:15.146
[SPEAKER_00]: plateau. And one thing that I've known that a lot of gliders come out in a different color when they go out there, probably because of the

29:15.166 --> 29:23.252
[SPEAKER_00]: volcanic sand. So I think hike and fly equipment is definitely not recommended. So if you can just throw some light on what kind of

29:23.292 --> 29:31.558
[SPEAKER_00]: equipment do you recommend visiting pilots to bring in? Yeah, so one of the main takeoffs now, we've got this green sort of fabric put on the

29:31.598 --> 29:40.163
[SPEAKER_01]: takeoff. And I think that's pretty kind to the winds. One of the earlier takeoffs that we had, which is very famous, Harrison's Polly, that is

29:40.423 --> 29:48.567
[SPEAKER_01]: almost completely covered with red dust. So if you take off from there or you land there, you know, guaranteed that your glider, you know, if you

29:48.607 --> 29:56.932
[SPEAKER_01]: come with a nice white colored alpine wing, it's going to go back red. And, you know, definitely it's going to affect your resale. So yeah,

29:57.092 --> 30:06.002
[SPEAKER_01]: don't come to Panspuni with your best wing. All of us local pilots, we have two wings. So we'll have our competition wings and the

30:06.042 --> 30:16.872
[SPEAKER_01]: beer-billing wing. And then we have a local wing, which is already red in color. But it doesn't really affect the material. You know, any rocks

30:16.952 --> 30:27.555
[SPEAKER_01]: that are in the area are mostly pebbles. So, you know, nicely rounded. There are no sharp rocks as such to kind of cut your glider or, you know,

30:27.595 --> 30:36.581
[SPEAKER_01]: snag the lines and things like that. There is quite a bit of grass even till now. So if you look at the start of the season in November, there are

30:36.621 --> 30:45.131
[SPEAKER_01]: actually photographs of green grass on the takeoffs. It's really gorgeous in November. You don't get very high, but the sheer visual

30:45.171 --> 30:55.681
[SPEAKER_01]: beauty of the place is fabulous. After the rainfall, if flying is not your only focus, Then we've got the karst plateau close by, which is a

30:55.741 --> 31:04.566
[SPEAKER_01]: World Heritage Site. You know, it's covered, it's green and lush with all kinds of flowers and things like that. But as it progresses into the

31:04.627 --> 31:13.271
[SPEAKER_01]: season, the grass kind of starts drying up, you know, slowly turns to yellow and then eventually kind of, you know, completely, you know,

31:13.291 --> 31:22.397
[SPEAKER_01]: around March, the locals start burning the grass. For what reason, I cannot tell them. But I think they do it to control accidental fires. So

31:22.417 --> 31:33.707
[SPEAKER_01]: they start burning regions. Late in March, you actually see entire hillside which are black in color. And that's not very nice. So yes, if

31:33.727 --> 31:43.012
[SPEAKER_01]: you just lay your wing down anywhere, then it's not nice. But there are enough places if you plan your landings. You know, we have this

31:43.052 --> 31:54.156
[SPEAKER_01]: beautiful lake called Om Dam. It's a lake, it's a dam on the Krishna River and which forms a V-shaped lake. And that's kind of our favorite spot.

31:55.136 --> 32:03.759
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you come, you land by the lake, you have laminar winds. Even if you land on the table land, the main table land of Panchpani, which we

32:03.799 --> 32:11.662
[SPEAKER_01]: normally do, because then it's just a short hike down to wherever you're staying. That's also quite forgiving, even late in the season. There

32:11.702 --> 32:19.906
[SPEAKER_01]: are patches of grass, not green grass, but yellowed out grass, where you can lay out your wing and pack it without it turning a different color.

32:21.046 --> 32:28.630
[SPEAKER_00]: Without Panch Ganesh putting a stamp on it saying that, yes, I've been here. Yes, but if you take over land on Harrison's Folly, it's

32:28.690 --> 32:38.187
[SPEAKER_01]: guaranteed that your wing is going to turn red. I think that's an achievement in itself. You basically kind of have earned it. Not a lot of

32:38.247 --> 32:47.309
[SPEAKER_00]: glider pilots can say that we have been to a place which gave a glider a whole different hue in a sense. Yeah, that's a good way to look at it.

32:47.690 --> 32:57.442
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but we've got a lot of people coming back. So I don't think anyone's really worried about the wing changing colors so much. Now, I think the

